 marine fish follows Wallace's Line: the phylogeography of the three-spot seahorse (<italic>Hippocampus trimaculatus</italic>, Syngnathidae, Teleostei) in Southeast Asia</article-title><source>Journal of Biogeography</source><year>2004</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>1975</fpage><lpage>1985</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1365-2699.2004.01153.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sulaiman</surname><given-names>ZH</given-names></name><name><surname>Ovenden</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Population genetic evidence for the east-west division of the Spanish mackerel (<italic>Scomberomorus commerson</italic>, Perciformes, Teleostei) along Wallace's Line</article-title><source>Biodiversity and Conservation</source><comment></comment></citation></ref><ref id="B22"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Walsh</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Metzger</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Higuchi</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Chelex 100 as a medium for simple extraction of DNA for PCR-based typing forensic material</article-title><source>Biotechniques</source><year>1991</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>506</fpage><lpage>513</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1867860</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B23"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Peakall</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Smouse</surname><given-names>PE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>GenAlEx 6: genetic analysis in Excel. Population genetic software for teaching and research</article-title><source>Molecular Ecology Notes</source><year>2006</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>288</fpage><lpage>295</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1471-8286.2005.01155.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B24"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pardini</surname><given-names>AT</given-names></name><name><surname>Jones</surname><given-names>CS</given-names></name><name><surname>Noble</surname><given-names>LR</given-names></name><name><surname>Kreiser</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Malcolm</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Bruce</surname><given-names>BD</given-names></name><name><surname>Stevens</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Cliff</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Scholl</surname><given-names>MC</given-names></name><name><surname>Francis</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>Sex-biased dispersal of great white sharks &#x02013; In some respects, these sharks behave more like whales and dolphins than other fish</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>2001</year><volume>412</volume><fpage>139</fpage><lpage>140</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11449258</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/35084125</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B25"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ovenden</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name><name><surname>Broderick</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Street</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Microsatellite primers for two carcharinid sharks (<italic>Carcharinus tilstoni </italic>and <italic>C. sorrah</italic>) and their usefulness across a wide range of shark species</article-title><source>Molecular Ecology Notes</source><year>2006</year><volume>6</volume><fpage>415</fpage><lpage>418</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1111/j.1471-8286.2005.01254.x</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B26"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"